1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,930
Two genders. Fifty-five genders.

2
00:00:03,010 --> 00:00:04,970
Hundreds of genders.

3
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:08,750
Before we start counting,
what exactly goes on the list?

4
00:00:09,419 --> 00:00:13,779
Imagine a form with three options:
woman, man, and non-binary.

5
00:00:14,039 --> 00:00:16,859
Now add another option: trans woman.

6
00:00:17,399 --> 00:00:19,199
Have we discovered another gender?

7
00:00:19,759 --> 00:00:21,759
Or added a more specific description?

8
00:00:22,449 --> 00:00:26,289
A trans woman can describe
herself as both trans and a woman.

9
00:00:27,049 --> 00:00:28,649
Those answers overlap.

10
00:00:29,329 --> 00:00:32,949
Counting the labels as separate
boxes already gives us a problem.

11
00:00:34,021 --> 00:00:39,021
Now imagine that someone replies: "But
humans reproduce with sperm and eggs."

12
00:00:39,841 --> 00:00:41,621
That's a statement about biology.

13
00:00:42,461 --> 00:00:45,701
It doesn't tell us how many
descriptions belong on that form.

14
00:00:46,387 --> 00:00:50,627
This is how an argument about a number
can hide an argument about a definition.

15
00:00:51,507 --> 00:00:54,087
One person counts reproductive categories.

16
00:00:54,867 --> 00:00:56,367
Another counts identities.

17
00:00:57,247 --> 00:00:58,947
Someone else counts words.

18
00:01:00,116 --> 00:01:02,296
There is a real example of the problem.

19
00:01:03,156 --> 00:01:06,316
In 2014, Facebook announced
sixty custom gender

20
00:01:06,546 --> 00:01:09,416
options for people using
its German interface.

21
00:01:10,219 --> 00:01:12,779
Sixty options in a profile menu.

22
00:01:13,439 --> 00:01:17,339
The announcement described a way for
people to express their identities.

23
00:01:18,159 --> 00:01:21,739
It wasn't the discovery of sixty
kinds of reproductive system.

24
00:01:21,994 --> 00:01:25,754
But saying "it's just a menu"
doesn't answer everything either.

25
00:01:26,514 --> 00:01:28,414
Why did people want those options?

26
00:01:29,094 --> 00:01:31,254
What were the original two boxes missing?

27
00:01:31,993 --> 00:01:35,133
To understand that, we need
to separate three things: the

28
00:01:35,363 --> 00:01:40,433
body, the expectations around it, and the
person living in it. They are connected.

29
00:01:41,193 --> 00:01:42,793
They aren't interchangeable.

30
00:01:43,927 --> 00:01:47,407
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

31
00:01:48,027 --> 00:01:49,627
It really helps the channel.

32
00:01:52,017 --> 00:01:53,617
Let's start with the body.

33
00:01:54,317 --> 00:01:56,257
Human sexual reproduction involves two

34
00:01:56,487 --> 00:01:59,397
kinds of reproductive
cell: sperm and eggs.

35
00:01:59,917 --> 00:02:01,677
These are called gametes.

36
00:02:02,123 --> 00:02:05,143
In reproductive biology,
the male role is associated

37
00:02:05,373 --> 00:02:08,343
with sperm, and the female role with eggs.

38
00:02:09,303 --> 00:02:12,743
That distinction gives the
categories a biological basis.

39
00:02:13,906 --> 00:02:18,226
It doesn't mean that every individual
must be producing those cells right now.

40
00:02:18,706 --> 00:02:21,046
A boy doesn't become male at puberty.

41
00:02:22,006 --> 00:02:24,566
An infertile man doesn't stop being male.

42
00:02:25,185 --> 00:02:29,245
Equally, a woman doesn't stop being
female when she reaches menopause.

43
00:02:30,025 --> 00:02:33,945
Reproductive organisation and current
fertility are different things.

44
00:02:34,269 --> 00:02:37,429
So the argument "some women
can't have children" doesn't,

45
00:02:37,659 --> 00:02:41,549
by itself, make biological
sex impossible to define.

46
00:02:42,609 --> 00:02:46,109
It shows why fertility alone is
a poor test for an individual.

47
00:02:46,469 --> 00:02:49,969
But the body has more going on
than two labels on a diagram.

48
00:02:50,709 --> 00:02:54,329
Sex development involves
chromosomes, genes, gonads,

49
00:02:54,409 --> 00:02:57,289
hormones, and the way
tissues respond to them.

50
00:02:57,809 --> 00:02:59,949
Usually, these produce a familiar pattern.

51
00:03:00,179 --> 00:03:04,689
XY chromosomes, testes,
and typically male anatomy.

52
00:03:05,169 --> 00:03:09,309
Or XX chromosomes, ovaries,
and typically female anatomy.

53
00:03:10,427 --> 00:03:12,487
Usually isn't the same as always.

54
00:03:13,307 --> 00:03:17,367
Consider a condition called complete
androgen insensitivity syndrome.

55
00:03:18,147 --> 00:03:19,907
The name is a mouthful.

56
00:03:20,387 --> 00:03:22,107
The mechanism matters more.

57
00:03:22,510 --> 00:03:26,950
Someone with this condition has XY
chromosomes and develops testes.

58
00:03:27,070 --> 00:03:31,390
But their cells do not respond to
androgens, including testosterone.

59
00:03:32,350 --> 00:03:36,269
The signal is there. The
cells cannot receive it.

60
00:03:36,349 --> 00:03:40,389
As a result, they are born with
typically female external genitals.

61
00:03:41,349 --> 00:03:45,109
A different hormone from the testes
prevents the uterus from developing.

62
00:03:45,969 --> 00:03:49,009
Chromosomes alone don't tell
you all of that anatomy.

63
00:03:50,054 --> 00:03:53,154
This is one example of a
difference in sex development.

64
00:03:53,914 --> 00:03:58,394
Intersex is an umbrella term used for
variations in sex characteristics.

65
00:03:59,254 --> 00:04:02,194
It doesn't describe one uniform body type.

66
00:04:02,805 --> 00:04:06,705
These variations don't introduce
a third kind of human gamete.

67
00:04:07,085 --> 00:04:11,785
Two reproductive roles can coexist
with variation in how bodies develop.

68
00:04:14,405 --> 00:04:17,585
Now move from the body to
the expectations around it.

69
00:04:18,185 --> 00:04:19,825
Who is supposed to be tough?

70
00:04:20,305 --> 00:04:22,025
Who is supposed to do the caring?

71
00:04:22,785 --> 00:04:25,145
What counts as masculine or feminine?

72
00:04:25,856 --> 00:04:28,056
These are questions about gender roles.

73
00:04:28,716 --> 00:04:32,916
They concern what a society
expects, encourages, or discourages.

74
00:04:33,436 --> 00:04:37,556
Those expectations can change
between places and over time.

75
00:04:38,337 --> 00:04:40,737
A man caring for his
children, or a woman who

76
00:04:40,967 --> 00:04:44,717
dislikes dresses, hasn't
automatically changed gender.

77
00:04:45,477 --> 00:04:50,017
Breaking a stereotype and identifying
as another gender are different things.

78
00:04:51,166 --> 00:04:55,466
Gender identity is a person's
internal sense of their own gender.

79
00:04:56,206 --> 00:05:00,466
For some people, being a man or a woman
fits the sex they were assigned at birth.

80
00:05:01,246 --> 00:05:03,046
For others, it doesn't.

81
00:05:03,511 --> 00:05:05,771
Cisgender describes that alignment.

82
00:05:06,311 --> 00:05:08,471
Transgender describes a gender identity

83
00:05:08,701 --> 00:05:11,351
that differs from the
sex assigned at birth.

84
00:05:12,151 --> 00:05:14,331
These words describe a relationship.

85
00:05:15,051 --> 00:05:17,391
They aren't names for extra gametes.

86
00:05:17,821 --> 00:05:20,361
A definition tells us what a word means.

87
00:05:21,041 --> 00:05:24,301
It doesn't explain where
gender identity comes from.

88
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:27,050
Non-binary is an umbrella
term for identities

89
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:30,350
that aren't exclusively man or woman.

90
00:05:31,050 --> 00:05:35,190
It doesn't mean that everyone using
it has exactly the same experience.

91
00:05:36,401 --> 00:05:38,841
Gender expression is how
someone presents themselves

92
00:05:39,071 --> 00:05:41,741
through clothing, appearance or behaviour.

93
00:05:42,661 --> 00:05:44,981
A skirt tells you what someone is wearing.

94
00:05:45,581 --> 00:05:49,101
It doesn't tell you their
chromosomes or their gender identity.

95
00:05:49,477 --> 00:05:51,737
So "gender" needs some context.

96
00:05:52,497 --> 00:05:56,977
Are we discussing social expectations,
personal identity, or presentation?

97
00:05:57,737 --> 00:06:01,537
A conversation can slide between
them without anyone noticing.

98
00:06:03,927 --> 00:06:06,227
Now we can return to those long lists.

99
00:06:07,107 --> 00:06:11,187
Imagine writing "woman" on one card
and "cisgender woman" on another.

100
00:06:11,547 --> 00:06:14,267
A third card says "transgender woman".

101
00:06:14,822 --> 00:06:16,602
We now have three cards.

102
00:06:16,782 --> 00:06:21,482
But the second and third are more specific
descriptions within the first category.

103
00:06:22,182 --> 00:06:25,502
Three labels do not automatically
mean three separate genders.

104
00:06:25,957 --> 00:06:29,517
So if someone says "fifty-five",
ask to see the list.

105
00:06:30,237 --> 00:06:32,197
You can count its entries exactly.

106
00:06:32,957 --> 00:06:35,637
But to count distinct genders,
you need to decide which

107
00:06:35,867 --> 00:06:38,677
labels overlap and which
name separate categories.

108
00:06:39,075 --> 00:06:44,075
And there's no universally agreed rule for
where one label stops and another begins.

109
00:06:45,246 --> 00:06:49,306
This is also why "there are
infinitely many" needs an explanation.

110
00:06:50,306 --> 00:06:53,526
An open-ended vocabulary isn't
a measurement of infinity.

111
00:06:54,286 --> 00:06:56,346
It means the list hasn't been closed.

112
00:06:57,165 --> 00:06:59,085
You might compare this with colours.

113
00:06:59,885 --> 00:07:03,345
Is turquoise a separate colour,
or a kind of blue or green?

114
00:07:03,725 --> 00:07:07,545
The answer depends partly on how
finely you divide your categories.

115
00:07:07,958 --> 00:07:09,878
But that comparison has a limit.

116
00:07:10,578 --> 00:07:12,558
It illustrates a problem with naming.

117
00:07:13,398 --> 00:07:15,718
It doesn't establish that
gender, or biological

118
00:07:15,948 --> 00:07:19,158
sex, is one smooth rainbow-shaped scale.

119
00:07:19,558 --> 00:07:22,358
An analogy isn't a biological model.

120
00:07:23,415 --> 00:07:27,115
"But people invented these words." Yes.

121
00:07:27,345 --> 00:07:29,695
Money depends on human rules too.

122
00:07:30,215 --> 00:07:32,315
That doesn't make your rent imaginary.

123
00:07:33,095 --> 00:07:34,975
A description can matter in someone's life

124
00:07:35,205 --> 00:07:37,835
without naming a new
reproductive category.

125
00:07:38,452 --> 00:07:42,372
Still, giving something a name
doesn't automatically explain it.

126
00:07:42,952 --> 00:07:46,972
We can ask what a term includes, and
whether it helps answer our question.

127
00:07:47,259 --> 00:07:49,679
That applies to short definitions too.

128
00:07:50,339 --> 00:07:53,099
Take the question: "What
makes someone a woman?"

129
00:07:53,499 --> 00:07:57,239
Before choosing a test, notice which
question you want it to answer.

130
00:07:57,519 --> 00:08:01,939
If you mean an adult human female,
you are using a sex-based definition.

131
00:08:03,019 --> 00:08:05,599
You then need to say what
you mean by female, and

132
00:08:05,829 --> 00:08:08,459
how you apply it to the
individual in front of you.

133
00:08:08,545 --> 00:08:11,185
If you mean a gender
identity, you are asking

134
00:08:11,415 --> 00:08:13,705
about the person's sense of being a woman.

135
00:08:14,485 --> 00:08:19,485
Answering that question with a chromosome
count changes the subject being measured.

136
00:08:20,563 --> 00:08:24,403
This doesn't make every disagreement
an innocent misunderstanding.

137
00:08:25,023 --> 00:08:28,063
People can understand the
distinction and still disagree

138
00:08:28,293 --> 00:08:31,603
about which category should
matter in a particular setting.

139
00:08:32,603 --> 00:08:36,203
A vocabulary lesson won't
settle all of those arguments.

140
00:08:37,043 --> 00:08:39,223
It can stop us treating
an answer to one question

141
00:08:39,453 --> 00:08:42,563
as an automatic answer to every other one.

142
00:08:43,669 --> 00:08:45,569
So how many genders are there?

143
00:08:46,229 --> 00:08:50,609
For human reproductive roles, we have
the distinction between sperm and eggs.

144
00:08:51,489 --> 00:08:55,629
For individual sex characteristics,
we also need to describe variation.

145
00:08:56,905 --> 00:08:59,445
For gender identities,
there isn't a single

146
00:08:59,675 --> 00:09:02,705
universally agreed number
of distinct categories.

147
00:09:03,445 --> 00:09:08,685
Man and woman don't describe everyone, and
broader and narrower labels can overlap.

148
00:09:09,819 --> 00:09:15,199
For the options on a particular form,
there is an exact answer. Count them.

149
00:09:15,739 --> 00:09:19,959
Just don't mistake the design of the form
for a discovery about the whole species.

150
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:25,280
The strange thing isn't that we have
so many words to describe people.

151
00:09:25,980 --> 00:09:28,900
It's that we expect one
number to describe bodies,

152
00:09:28,980 --> 00:09:32,100
identities, and the rules
we build around them.

153
00:09:33,003 --> 00:09:38,173
Before answering "two" or "fifty-five",
finish the question. How many... of what?
